(project pricklypear-ukulele
 (doc "Ukulele theory as a MECHANISM, not a chord dictionary. Companion to borge/songs.borg: songs owns the instrument-free pitch algebra (spelled pitch classes, the finite quality table, keys, roman numerals, key-fit); this chapter owns the instrument. The thesis: on four reentrant strings the authoritative object is not 'a chord' but a (pitch-class set, playable shape) pair. Chord names are INTERPRETATIONS of a set, next-chord suggestions are OPERATORS on a progression, and styles (jazz, math rock, worship, pop) are WEIGHT PROFILES over one shared move grammar — never separate theories. Three structural facts drive everything: reentrancy makes bass a property of the shape rather than the string; four strings cap a voicing at four distinct pitch classes, so every extended chord is an OMISSION decision; and the shape space is finite and small enough to enumerate exactly at read time. Everything here is pure Nopales over songs L0, REPL-testable through the local lens, with faces as renderings of the same values.")
 (status partial)
 (convention nopales-package)
 (* agent note (|2026-10-23 M1 make pass (libs/uke/lib.pp + additive libs/songs/lib.pp quality rows): geometry, target-driven shape search, reverse naming, and chord voicings are implemented and were exercised on the dev image (127.0.0.1:18080, admin/personal). Acceptance cases: 0000 -> Am7/C6, 0003 -> C, 4400 -> Asus2/Esus4/Esus, 1212 -> the four dim7 names, 1000 -> AmMaj7 (after extending songs/qualities 15 -> 28), key context Am -> Am7 and C -> C6. DESIGN CHANGE from the first draft: materializing all 5,993 shapes busts the eval step budget, so (uke/shapes-for-pcs) solves per string (only frets that sound a wanted pc) and crosses the four small lists; a whole-neck enumeration is M2 diagnostic work. Not promoted to system/main yet — the definitions are pinned on admin/personal, the vault file is the source. M2 (also 2026-10-23): the finite move grammar and (uke/next), with roman-annotated cards, sounding-set dedupe, flat-spelled tritone subs, and ii-V cadence detection; verified E7/G/C in the Am-Dm case and C [cadence] on Dm-G7.|) *)

 (section thesis
  (doc "Three claims. (1) NAME IS NOT IDENTITY: a fret vector determines a pitch-class set; that set may have several correct names (0000 is both Am7 and C6; a dim7 shape has four roots). The mechanism therefore returns ALL interpretations with provenance, and never pretends one name is the chord. (2) MOVES BEAT LISTS: 'what comes next' is generated by a finite, named grammar of harmonic operators (diatonic successors, secondary dominants, tritone substitution, diminished approach, modal interchange, chromatic mediants, planing, sus/drop-3 color, pedal) applied to the current chord and key. Every candidate carries a one-line explanation, so the surface teaches rather than oracles. (3) STYLE IS WEIGHTING: jazz and math rock are not different chord tables. Jazz reharmonizes FUNCTIONS (ii-V insertion, tritone subs, guide-tone voicings); math rock transforms SHAPES (planing, open-string pedals, dropped thirds, quartal stacks, odd meter). Both are operators on the same representation; a style profile reweights moves, biases qualities, and filters shapes. No ML is required or wanted: the system must be arguable from first principles. The two product goals are solo chord-melody arrangement — melody and harmony are ONE joint search, not two problems — and generated technique drills (formby, fingerstyle, legato), which are paths through the same shape graph.")
  (status planned)
  (verify
   (test "every suggestion returned by (uke/next ...) names the move that produced it and the input chord it transforms")
   (test "removing a style profile leaves the same candidate SET and only changes ranking; a profile can never invent an unnamed move")))

 (section geometry
  (doc "The instrument is data, not a constant buried in a renderer. Standard tuning gCEA in PHYSICAL string order (G C E A) is MIDI (67 60 64 69). Because the G string is high, the actual ascending pitch order is C4 E4 G4 A4 = (60 64 67 69); adjacent intervals are 4, 3, 2 semitones. Consequences the algebra must encode: (a) the lowest sounding pitch of a shape is argmin of its midis, NOT string 4 and NOT the root — slash-chord notation is mostly advisory on a reentrant uke; (b) any three adjacent strings span at most 7 semitones and all four span 9, so open-position voicings are inherently close and wide voicings cost stretch; (c) unisons and octave doublings are available and musically useful (open E4 and C-string fret 4 are the same pitch; open C4 and G-string fret 5 are octaves), so duplicate pitch classes are THICKNESS, not a parse error; (d) a fret vector is (frets G C E A) as integers, canonical value a proper list, with the four-digit string accepted as UI shorthand (the comma form handles frets above 9). The tuning table is a list keyed by name so low-G, baritone, and capo variants are data additions, never code branches.")
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (test "(uke/midis (2 0 0 0)) = (69 60 64 69) and (uke/lowest (uke/midis (2 0 0 0))) = 60; the A on the G string is audible but not the bass")
   (test "(uke/midis (4 4 0 0)) contains E4 twice (unison doubling) and (uke/names \"4400\") still names Esus4 and Asus2, not an error")
   (test "(uke/parse-frets \"0,10,0,3\") = (0 10 0 3); frets above 9 are representable even though the four-digit shorthand is only 0..9")))

 (section shape-space
  (doc "Generate the instrument, do not hand-maintain it. The reachable space at window 12 with span<=4 is 5,993 shapes covering 747 distinct pitch-class sets (495 four-note, 208 three-note) — but the engine does NOT materialize it on the hot path. For a target pitch-class set, (uke/shapes-for-pcs pcs) lets each string visit ONLY frets that sound a wanted pc and takes the cross-product, so the search is a few hundred vectors; (uke/voicings chord) wraps it. A whole-neck enumeration (uke/shapes window) remains a diagnostic for the neighborhood graph in M2. Playability in M1 is span (max fretted fret minus min fretted fret, opens ignored) at most 4 and all four strings sounding; fretted/finger/barre budgets are M3 knobs, and a muted string is a later -1 extension. The existing songs/uke-voicings table stops being the source of truth: it remains the CURATED PREFERRED shape per chord for chart rendering, while (uke/voicings chord) searches the generated space. A chord whose pc set exceeds four notes (9, 11, 13) has no exact shape; M3 adds COVER mode — select four chord tones by a declared priority (root and guide tones 3 and 7 first, then 5, then extensions) and report the omission set with the shape.")
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (test "(count (uke/voicings (songs/parse-chord \"C\"))) is 32 and every returned shape's pc set equals C major")
   (test "(uke/shapes-for-pcs (list 4 9 11)) contains (4 4 0 0) and every returned shape sounds exactly E A B")
   (test "(uke/voicings (songs/parse-chord \"C9\")) returns () in M1; the face says cover mode is not yet available and never claims to sound all five tones")))

 (section naming
  (doc "Reverse lookup is exact set matching, then declared relaxation. Build a 336-entry index once per session from songs/qualities for all 12 roots (28 qualities x 12 roots; memoized as a pure session delay): sorted pitch-class set -> list of (root, quality). (uke/names frets [key]) computes the input set and returns the FIRST non-empty class: (1) EXACT matches, same cardinality, all of them, because ambiguity is data; (2) SUBSET matches where the input is a named chord with omitted degrees (a three-note shell of a seventh); (3) SUPERSET matches where the input is a named chord plus a tone that is a legal extension from that root (add9, add11, 6, 7), reported as add/omit; (4) nearest matches with symmetric difference 1 when nothing else fits, labelled approximate. Returning only the winning class keeps a four-note exact shape from drowning in legal-but-redundant aliases; a verbose mode can expose the rest later. Ranking of the canonical name uses supplied key context first (songs/fit), then root-equals-key-tonic, then root-equals-lowest-pc, then a curated commonness order; with no key, commonness and bass win. Spelling uses the simple-accidental preference (songs/spell-in-key is the M2 refinement for key-aware spelling). Symmetric sets are flagged: dim7 has exactly 3 distinct pc sets on the whole instrument (each with 4 roots) and aug has 4 (each with 3 roots), so a single dim7 shape carries four correct names — this is the formal reason a diminished chord is a free connector, not folklore. songs/qualities is extended IN PLACE with the jazz morphemes (m6, mMaj7, maj9, m9, 11, 13, 7sus4, 7b5, 7#5, 7b9, 7#9, 6/9, add11) so there is still ONE finite table shared with songs.")
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (test "(uke/names \"0000\") contains both Am7 and C6; (uke/name \"0003\") is C; key context C ranks C6 first and key context Am ranks Am7 first")
   (test "(uke/names \"4400\") contains both Esus4 and Asus2: the same three pitch classes, two roots, one shape")
   (test "(uke/names \"1212\") returns exactly the four dim7 names Ddim7 Fdim7 G#dim7 Bdim7 with the symmetric flag set")
   (test "(uke/name \"1000\") is AmMaj7 after the quality-table extension, not 'Am (add G#)'")
   (test "a non-chord shape returns nearest matches labelled approximate, never an empty list and never a fabricated exact name")))

 (section neighborhood
  (doc "The uke-native discovery surface: move a SHAPE, then ask what it became. Build a graph over generated shapes with edges for single-finger moves (one string changes fret by -2..+2, others fixed), slides (all fretted strings shift by the same amount), and open-string pedals (one string returns to 0 while others move). Each node exposes its name set from the naming section, so the graph is simultaneously a chord-finding tool and a voice-leading map. Symmetry becomes visible rather than mystical: sliding a dim7 shape by 3 frets returns the SAME pc set under a different root name, and sliding an aug shape by 4 does the same; the neck repeats. This section is the formal answer to 'I can operate completely off string proximity' — proximity is an edge in a finite, inspectable graph, not a vibe.")
  (status planned)
  (verify
   (test "the slide edge from a dim7 shape at frets f to f+3 is a self-loop in pc-set space and a different name in spelling space")
   (test "every neighborhood edge reports the finger delta and the resulting name set in one value; no edge is a hidden retune")))

 (section moves
  (doc "A finite grammar of pure operators over (chord, key). Each returns (glyph move reason roman strength sounding-set), where strength is profile-independent plausibility. DIATONIC: the weighted degree-function table for major and natural minor (I->{IV,V,vi,ii}, ii->{V,vii}, V->{I,vi}, etc.) PLUS every remaining diatonic chord at a low weight, so a suggestion set never silently omits a legal degree. SECONDARY-DOMINANT: V7 of every scale degree. TRITONE-SUB: the dominant seventh a tritone away from each secondary dominant, spelled flat (Bb7, not A#7). DIM-APPROACH: a dim7 rooted a semitone below each degree (leading-tone) or a semitone above (upper); because only three dim7 sets exist, the candidate list is deduped by SOUNDING SET, so the three sets appear once each rather than twelve spellings. MODAL-INTERCHANGE: the same degree in the parallel mode. CHROMATIC-MEDIANT and PLANING: the same quality on roots shifted by +/-3/+/-4 and +/-1/+/-2 semitones respectively. COLOR: quality recolors on the same root (7->9/7sus4/7b9, minor->m7/m6/m9, major->sus4/sus2/add9/maj7, dim->m7b5/dim7). CADENCE: a detected ii-V history adds the tonic resolution above the plain diatonic tonic. (uke/next history key) unions all candidates, dedupes by sounding pitch-class set, scores by move strength plus key fit, and sorts descending; every card carries its move name, reason, and roman numeral. Style weighting and the remaining n-gram cliches (I-vi-IV-V) are M6; proximity cost is M3.")
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (test "(uke/next (list Am Dm) Am) includes E7 as V, G and C as diatonic degrees, and a dim7 approach to the next tonic; every card carries a move name and roman numeral")
   (test "(uke/next (list Dm G7) C) puts C [cadence] above the plain diatonic C, and the ii-V candidacy is detected from history, not the last chord alone")
   (test "the three distinct dim7 sets appear once each after sounding-set dedupe, never as twelve symmetric spellings")))

 (section styles
  (doc "A profile is DATA: (name quality-bias move-weights voicing-rules meter-rules). JAZZ: extensions up, guide-tone voicings (3 and 7), rootless shapes, ii-V insertion, secondary dominants, tritone subs, chromatic ii-V. MATH ROCK: shape transforms first (plane, drop-3, quartal stacks, open-string pedals), sus/add9/no3 quality bias, odd-meter and displacement rules; harmonic function is a weaker signal than geometric voice leading. WORSHIP: sus, IV/V, modal interchange, big open shapes, slow harmonic rhythm. POP: diatonic, I-V-vi-IV, strong root motion. A profile never adds a move that is not in the moves section; it reweights the existing grammar and filters shapes. Profiles compose by multiplying weights, which makes 'jazz plus math rock' a value, not a new subsystem. (uke/reharmonize progression profile) applies a profile to an existing progression and returns N alternative progressions, each annotated with the exact moves applied — variations are explained edits, not opaque generations.")
  (status planned)
  (verify
   (test "every profile's move-weight keys are a subset of the grammar's move names; an unknown key is a load-time error naming the accepted set")
   (test "composing jazz and math-rock weights is commutative and associative on the candidate scores")
   (test "reharmonizing a diatonic four-bar under pop returns the input as the highest-ranked path")))

 (section proximity
  (doc "String proximity is a metric, so arranging is an optimization. For shapes v and w: cost = sum over strings of |fret(v,i) - fret(w,i)| + shift-penalty when the fretted window moves more than 3 frets + barre-penalty + mute-penalty - open-string-bonus when a ringing open string is retained. (uke/voicings chord near) returns shapes ranked by that cost against a reference. (uke/arrange progression [profile]) runs a Viterbi pass over the per-chord shape sets, choosing one shape per chord to minimize total movement across the whole progression, and reports the path, the total cost, and the worst hand jump. This is the feature that makes the mechanism feel like an instrument rather than a dictionary: C 0003 to Am7 0000 is one lifted finger, and the arranger can prefer that over a theoretically prettier but physically jumpy voicing. The cost function is data-shaped so a player can tune shift and barre penalties without touching the search.")
  (status planned)
  (verify
   (test "(uke/arrange (list C Am F G)) returns four shapes and a total cost no greater than the sum of each chord's individually nearest shape to the previous one")
   (test "changing only the shift penalty changes the chosen path for at least one progression, proving the metric is live rather than decorative")
   (test "the arranger never returns a shape outside the playability budget and never silently drops a chord")))

 (section solo-arrangement
  (doc "Solo ukulele is a JOINT melody/harmony search, not two separate problems. A melody voice is already a score value (the songs m: line; scripts/pp-transcribe.py can draft one from audio), so the arrangement input is (melody, chart-or-key). At every tick, choose a shape AND designate which string carries the melody. Constraints: (1) the melody pc must sound at its tick; (2) the designated string must be the highest sounding voice at that moment, or the path pays a clarity penalty — on reentrant uke 'highest' is computed from midis, never assumed to be the A string; (3) the shape must satisfy the playability budget; (4) the harmony must be legal — a chord from the chart, a candidate from the move grammar, or a chord that contains the melody note as a chord tone. A non-chord melody note is allowed only when labelled a tension (9, 11, 13) or a passing tone. The search is a Viterbi pass over (tick, shape) with the proximity cost plus melody-continuity and clarity terms; the k-best paths ARE the variations. CROSS-KEY RECONCILIATION is the named use case: (uke/keys melody [reference]) ranks all 24 keys by duration-weighted diatonic fit and names the relationship to the reference (relative minor, plus or minus n semitones, modal shift); (uke/reconcile melody chart) searches the alignment — transpose the chart, keep it and reharmonize in place, or hybrid — that maximizes melody notes covered by chord tones, then reports every chord actually used that the source chart did not name, via reverse naming. That is the 'the lead was in a different key and I ended up with chords that were not in the chart' case: the engine names what you played and explains why it works. NOTATION is a rendering of the chosen path: (uke/tab arrangement) emits four string lines with frets, chord names above, the melody marked, and legato markers (h/p) wherever the path changes one string's fret by 1-2 without a pluck. Nothing is stored.")
  (status planned)
  (verify
   (test "every shape in a returned solo path sounds the melody pc at its tick; a non-chord melody note is returned with a tension or passing label, never silently accepted")
   (test "the designated melody string is the highest sounding string in the shape unless the path carries the clarity-penalty flag")
   (test "(uke/reconcile melody chart) transposed to the melody key and reconciled in place agree on the chord FUNCTIONS; only the invented-chord report differs")
   (smoke "the Kadosh m: line produces a four-line tab; a synth line a minor third away reconciles and lists the chords the source chart never named")))

 (section drills
  (doc "Technique drills are GENERATED from the same graph, not stored as a content library. A drill is (family, seed, progression, right-hand pattern, tempo ladder, constraints). FORMBY: split-stroke and syncopated chord-melody. The right hand is a tick-level template (thumb on strong beats, index on offbeats, optional muted chuck); the left hand is a progression chosen for rapid changes, dim7/aug connectors, and chromatic movement, supplied by the move grammar plus the corpus prior. Difficulty knobs: harmonic rhythm, syncopation density, chord-change proximity, key. FINGERSTYLE: arpeggio patterns over shapes (p-i-m-a, travis, string skips) with the melody pinned to the top voice; knobs: pattern length, thumb independence, position shift, open-string pedal. LEGATO (math rock): paths through the neighborhood graph where every transition is a one-string fret change of 1-2 with no pluck — literally hammer-on/pull-off material; knobs: leap size, string crossing, odd meter (5/4, 7/8), subdivision, shape-shift distance. A fourth family, INVERSIONS, is a prerequisite rather than a style: one chord across every shape on the neck, then the same harmony with the melody moved from string to string, ranked by proximity. It is a filter over the shape space, not new theory, and it directly trains the skill that solo arrangement demands. Every drill is a score (progressions plus pattern plus ticks), so the existing renderer, tab face, and tempo ladder apply unchanged. Reproducibility: seed plus knobs regenerate the drill byte-identically. Practice state — best clean tempo, last played, family, weakness tags — is the ONE new row type (ukepractice), because it is user state, not derived music. A weakness profile is a filter over families: saying 'I am bad at pull-offs' selects legato drills whose edges are pull-off transitions, not a different theory.")
  (status planned)
  (verify
   (test "the same seed and knobs produce an identical drill score; no drill definition is stored as a row")
   (test "every legato transition changes exactly one string by 1-2 frets and carries h or p direction; a transition that needs a pluck is rejected")
   (smoke "a 7/8 legato drill renders tab and renders audio through the existing engine; the tempo ladder advances without regenerating the score")))

 (section local-first
  (doc "Local eval is the primary loop, so purity is a contract. Every function in this chapter takes VALUES — chord, key, shape, melody, progression list — and touches no DB, request, session, or clock. Faces are the only I/O layer: they read rows once and pass values down; the engine never calls rows, request-param, or current-author from inside a computation. Index builds (shape space, name index, quality table) are pure and memoized on the content hash of (tuning, window, budget, quality-table), seedable through the lens. Corpus priors take the progression list as an argument; a face may read chordsong rows, but the engine does not. This is what makes the REPL the first face and the browser a rendering, not the other way around. Acceptance: M1-M4 run end to end under pp-local with the network down.")
  (status planned)
  (verify
   (smoke "the M1-M4 acceptance cases run under pp-local with PP_URL unreachable and produce identical values")
   (test "no engine function calls rows, request-param, current-author, or whoami; a stub that throws on those prims leaves pure calls working")
   (test "building the shape index twice returns identical values and the memo key is the declared content hash")))

 (section corpus
  (doc "The songbook is a corpus, and the habitat already stores it: every chordsong row is a real progression. Derive transition statistics at read time — counts by roman-numeral pair within a key and by (quality, root-interval) — and use them as a PRIOR alongside the grammar, never as authority. This answers the descriptive half of 'what would somebody use next' while the grammar answers the prescriptive half; the face shows both and labels which is which. Statistics are projections: memoized on the content hash of the songbook set, never stored as rows (the songs program-authority invariant). With a thin songbook the prior is weak and the grammar dominates; as charts accumulate, the empirical layer earns weight automatically. No embeddings, no learned model, no opaque score: every prior is a count a human can inspect and argue with.")
  (status planned)
  (verify
   (test "corpus counts for the Kadosh chart reproduce its actual transitions and are identical across two reads an hour apart")
   (test "deleting the corpus rows falls back to grammar-only ranking with no error and no stale counts")
   (test "no data_rows type stores transition counts, weights, or derived names")))

 (section faces
  (doc "One explorer face, GET-query state, pure function of (query, rows), same htmx fragment discipline as songs. /uke?frets=0003: name cards for every interpretation with ambiguity chips, a fret diagram whose actual lowest pitch is marked, add/omit provenance, and the curvature back to the chart when a key is supplied. /uke?prog=Am|Dm|Am|Esus: next-chord cards grouped by move family, each with a diagram, the one-line why, and the finger delta from the current shape; style toggles reweight without re-fetching rows. /uke?shape=0000: the neighborhood graph, with slides and single-finger moves as edges and the resulting names on each node. /uke?prog=...&target=C: the arranged progression, one shape per chord, total movement. /uke?rev=1: ear-training mode that hides the names until reveal. The page is private like /songs; it reads songs L0 and writes nothing. Solo and drill panels are the same discipline: /uke?melody=...&chart=... shows the chosen path as tab plus the invented-chord report; /uke?drill=legato&seed=7&tempo=90 renders a generated exercise with its tempo ladder. Every panel degrades to text (name, frets) when a shape is unplayable, never a blank cell.")
  (status planned)
  (verify
   (smoke "/uke?frets=0000 renders both Am7 and C6 with diagrams and marks C as the actual lowest pitch")
   (smoke "/uke?prog=Am|Dm|Am|Esus&style=jazz reorders the next-chord cards and every card still names its move")
   (test "two identical GETs are byte-identical an hour apart; no explorer view writes a row")))

 (section non-goals
  (doc "v1 ships NO learned model, NO embeddings, and NO opaque ranking; NO DSP in this chapter — melodies come from songs m: lines or a pp-transcribe draft, so the chapter consumes PITCH and never audio; NO invented tune — the arranger harmonizes a supplied melody and never composes one; NO full alternate-tuning or capo UI (the tuning table is data, the UI waits); NO muted/ghost-string notation until the -1 extension is specified; NO claim that a chord name is unique — ambiguity is the surface, not a bug; NO persisted derived shapes, names, corpus statistics, or drill definitions — the sole new row type is ukepractice (user progress); NO replacement of songs/uke-voicings (it stays the curated preferred shape for chart rendering); TAB as an INPUT parser remains a songs face, while the tab here is an OUTPUT rendering of a chosen path. A suggestion is never 'correct': it is a named, inspectable move.")
  (status planned)
  (verify
   (test "the chapter adds no kernel primitive and no data_rows type except ukepractice; borge drift after the first make pass shows pure package work")
   (test "removing every style profile still yields a usable explorer: names, moves, and arrangements remain")))

 (section sequencing
  (doc "M1 (SHIPPED 2026-10-23) PURE CORE: tuning table, target-driven shape search, reverse naming, and the extended quality table, in libs/uke/lib.pp over songs L0; REPL acceptance is the 0000 / 0003 / 4400 / 1212 / 1000 cases. M2 (SHIPPED 2026-10-23) MOVES: the operator grammar and (uke/next) with explanations, roman numerals, and sounding-set dedupe, still REPL-only. M3 PROXIMITY: cost metric, cover-mode voicing search, and (uke/arrange) via Viterbi. M4 SOLO: key ranking, cross-key reconciliation, the melody-on-string search, and tab output — the user-facing reason the mechanism exists. M5 DRILLS: formby, fingerstyle, legato, and inversion generators plus ukepractice state. M6 FACE + STYLES + CORPUS: the /uke explorer, profiles, reharmonize, the songbook prior, and ear-training. Each milestone is a pure read over the previous one; no milestone mutates the songbook except through the existing chordsong editor. The REPL is the first face throughout: M1-M5 are usable through pp-local before any browser work.")
  (status partial)
  (verify
   (test "borge report flips this chapter's sections from planned to implemented one milestone at a time")
   (test "after M1, libs/songs is unchanged except for the additive quality-table rows and the roman-detail/triad-class switches that name them; the uke package owns every instrument-specific value")))
)
